      <memberdef kind="function" id="group__emaclite_1ga2799884dbcd4e24e3f7fda067d457567" prot="public" static="no" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>u32</type>
        <definition>u32 EmacLitePhyDetect</definition>
        <argsstring>(XEmacLite *InstancePtr)</argsstring>
        <name>EmacLitePhyDetect</name>
        <param>
          <type><ref refid="struct_x_emac_lite" kindref="compound">XEmacLite</ref> *</type>
          <declname>InstancePtr</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Detects the PHY address by looking for successful MII status register contents (PHY register 1). </para>        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>It looks for a PHY that supports auto-negotiation and 10Mbps full-duplex and half-duplex. So, this code won&apos;t work for PHYs that don&apos;t support those features, but it&apos;s a bit more general purpose than matching a specific PHY manufacturer ID.</para><para>Note also that on some (older) Xilinx ML4xx boards, PHY address 0 does not properly respond to this query. But, since the default is 0 and assuming no other address responds, then it seems to work OK.</para><para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">InstancePtr</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>is the pointer to the instance of EmacLite driver.</para></parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
